DVSM :: LAYERED EXECUTION + VERIFICATION ORDERING MODEL
===============================================================================

## PRIMARY CORRECTION

CONSENSUS IS NOT FINAL.

Consensus (L5) only resolves:
- disagreement between nodes
- network alignment of computed states

It does NOT define:
- correctness of computation
- validity of simulation logic
- structural truth of state

Final correctness is defined by:
    L1–L4 deterministic execution + S_ECHO identity consistency

===============================================================================
I. STRICT LAYER EXECUTION ORDER (CORE PIPELINE)
===============================================================================

All DVSM-compatible engines execute in this strict dependency order:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(1) L1 — EXECUTION KERNEL (Ξ)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Input:
    S(t), I(t)

Output:
    S(t+1)

Role:
- deterministic state evolution
- fixed-point simulation engine

Dependency:
- NONE (root of system)

Used by:
- ALL engines (mandatory foundation)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(2) L3 — TOTAL ORDER INPUT LAYER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- imposes deterministic ordering on all external inputs
- guarantees identical execution sequence across nodes

Dependency:
- feeds into L1

Used by:
- ECS engines
- networked simulation systems
- DUME ingestion pipeline
- anti-cheat event systems

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(3) L4 — STATE IDENTITY FUNCTION (S_ECHO)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- produces canonical hash of system state

Dependency:
- L1 output state

Used by:
- rollback systems
- audit systems
- consensus comparison
- integrity verification in ALL engines

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(4) L5 — CONSENSUS LAYER (NON-FINAL COORDINATION)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- compares S_ECHO outputs across nodes
- determines agreement, not correctness

IMPORTANT RULE:

    Consensus ≠ Truth
    Consensus = Network Agreement on Execution Result

Output:
- accept / reject alignment signal
- triggers rollback or replay if divergence detected

Dependency:
- L4 (S_ECHO only)

Used by:
- multiplayer synchronization
- distributed simulation engines
- anti-cheat validation layers
- replication systems

===============================================================================
II. OPTIONAL VERIFICATION LAYERS (NON-EXECUTION CRITICAL)
===============================================================================

These layers DO NOT affect L1–L5 execution results.

They are used for inspection, auditing, and structural analysis.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(5) L7 — PARTIAL STATE VERIFICATION (MERKLE LAYER)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- verify subsets of state without full recomputation

Dependency:
- L4 or snapshot of L1 state

Used by:
- DUME engine (compression + audit)
- shard-based systems
- large-scale distributed ECS
- forensic replay systems

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(6) L8 — STRUCTURAL INTEGRITY LAYER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- validates ECS graph structure consistency

Dependency:
- L1 state topology

Used by:
- anti-cheat systems (entity injection detection)
- persistent world simulation engines
- memory integrity validation layers

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(7) L9 — DRIFT DETECTION LAYER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- detects divergence trends before failure

Dependency:
- L4 history stream

Used by:
- network simulation monitoring
- hardware nondeterminism detection
- long-running deterministic systems

===============================================================================
III. L10 / L∞ — GOVERNANCE + HISTORY LAYERS
===============================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
L10 — PROTOCOL EVOLUTION LAYER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- manages version transitions of deterministic rules

Constraint:
- must preserve S_ECHO equivalence classes

Used by:
- engine upgrades
- distributed system versioning
- live-service deterministic updates

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
L∞ — SEMANTIC HISTORY LAYER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Role:
- immutable execution history ledger

Used by:
- audit trails
- forensic reconstruction
- compliance systems

===============================================================================
IV. ENGINE DEPENDENCY MATRIX (IMPORTANT)
===============================================================================

Different systems consume different layers:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A. CORE ECS / GAME ENGINE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uses:
- L1 (simulation)
- L3 (input ordering)
- L4 (state identity)
- optional L5 (multiplayer sync)

Ignores:
- L7–L∞ unless debugging or replay mode

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
B. MULTIPLAYER / NETWORKED SIMULATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uses:
- L1–L5 fully

Optional:
- L7 for bandwidth optimization
- L9 for drift detection

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C. ANTI-CHEAT / COMPETITIVE INTEGRITY SYSTEMS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uses:
- L1 (authoritative simulation)
- L4 (state fingerprinting)
- L5 (client-server agreement validation)
- L8 (entity structure validation)

Purpose:
- detect tampering
- detect replay injection
- validate deterministic fairness

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D. DUME / DATA-DRIVEN INTELLIGENCE ENGINE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uses:
- L3 (ordered ingestion)
- L4 (identity binding of embeddings)
- L7 (compressed verification)
- L9 (drift detection)

Purpose:
- ensure deterministic dataset evolution
- audit embedding stability
- enforce reproducibility of transformations

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
E. GOVERNANCE / AUDIT SYSTEMS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uses:
- L4 (identity)
- L7 (proofs)
- L10 (version transitions)
- L∞ (history ledger)

Purpose:
- compliance
- traceability
- system evolution validation

===============================================================================
V. FINAL CORRECTED SEMANTIC MODEL
===============================================================================

OLD (incorrect framing):
    Consensus defines correctness

NEW (correct DVSM model):

    Correctness is defined by deterministic execution:

        S(t+1) = Ξ(S(t), I(t))

    Identity is defined by:

        S_ECHO(S)

    Consensus defines only:

        agreement(S_ECHO across nodes)

    Verification layers define:
        observability + integrity + drift detection

===============================================================================
VI. KEY ARCHITECTURAL INSIGHT
===============================================================================

DVSM separates three independent concerns:

1. EXECUTION (L1–L4)
   → what the system does

2. AGREEMENT (L5)
   → what nodes agree happened

3. OBSERVABILITY (L7–L∞)
   → how we verify and analyze what happened

These are intentionally decoupled so:
- simulation remains deterministic
- networking remains resilient
- verification remains optional and scalable

===============================================================================
END OF ORDERING SPECIFICATION
===============================================================================
===============================================================================
DVSM :: MULTI-RUNTIME GATEWAY + PORTING SPECIFICATION
C++ (Linux) / Swift (Client + Governance) / Shared Deterministic Core
===============================================================================

## PURPOSE

This document defines how the DVSM kernel is deployed consistently across:

- C++ (authoritative simulation runtime on Linux servers)
- Swift (client-side prediction, audit, governance tooling)
- Shared deterministic ABI contract (Ξ + S_ECHO + command stream)

Goal:
Ensure identical state evolution across all platforms.

===============================================================================
I. SHARED EXECUTION CONTRACT (PLATFORM-INDEPENDENT CORE)
===============================================================================

All implementations MUST conform to this invariant:

    S(t+1) = Ξ(S(t), I(t))

Where:
- S = deterministic ECS state
- I = ordered input stream (L3)
- Ξ = fixed-point execution kernel

State identity:

    S_ECHO(S) → uint64 hash

Equivalence rule:

    S_A == S_B ⇔ S_ECHO(S_A) == S_ECHO(S_B)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REQUIRED ABI RULES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Across ALL languages:

- fixed-point arithmetic only (Q32.32 or equivalent)
- deterministic iteration order
- no floating-point dependency in simulation core
- no OS-time dependency in kernel execution
- identical hashing implementation (FNV-1a or equivalent frozen spec)

===============================================================================
II. C++ (LINUX) AUTHORITY RUNTIME
===============================================================================

ROLE:
Primary deterministic simulation engine

RESPONSIBILITIES:
- Executes Ξ kernel
- Maintains ECS state
- Performs rollback and replay
- Generates canonical S_ECHO hashes
- Acts as consensus authority node

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORE MODULES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/kernel
  - execution.cpp        (Ξ engine)
  - ecs.cpp              (entity system)
  - order_stream.cpp     (L3 input sequencing)
  - echo.cpp             (S_ECHO implementation)

/net
  - consensus.cpp        (L5 node agreement layer)
  - replication.cpp      (state sync)

/audit (optional)
  - snapshot.cpp         (L7–L9 integration hooks)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXECUTION LOOP

while (running) {
    apply_ordered_input_stream();
    step_state();                 // Ξ
    hash = S_ECHO(state);
    broadcast(hash);
    resolve_consensus();
}

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GUARANTEE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

C++ runtime is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for simulation execution.

===============================================================================
III. SWIFT CLIENT + GOVERNANCE GATEWAY
===============================================================================

ROLE:
Prediction, validation, audit interface, and secure client execution mirror

NOT authoritative for simulation

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORE MODULES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/DVSMClient
  - DVSMState.swift
  - DVSMCommandBuffer.swift
  - DVSMHashValidator.swift

/AuditLayer
  - DVSMIntegrityChecker.swift
  - DVSMReplayVerifier.swift

/Networking
  - DVSMTransport.swift

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLIENT EXECUTION MODEL

Swift runs:

- predicted local Ξ simulation (optional)
- receives authoritative C++ state hashes
- validates S_ECHO consistency
- performs rollback correction if divergence detected

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SYNC RULE

if local_S_ECHO != server_S_ECHO:
    request_state_replay()

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
USE CASES

- anti-cheat validation client
- UI simulation rendering
- governance audit dashboards
- forensic replay tools

===============================================================================
IV. CONSENSUS GATEWAY (L5 CROSS-SYSTEM BRIDGE)
===============================================================================

Consensus operates ONLY at synchronization boundaries.

It is NOT part of simulation correctness.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FLOW

C++ nodes compute:

    S_ECHO(state)

Then:

    broadcast hashes → cluster

Consensus result:

    agreement map of hashes (not state authority)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT RULE

Consensus outcome:

    does NOT override Ξ execution
    does NOT modify state
    does NOT resolve correctness disputes

It only signals:

    convergence OR divergence

===============================================================================
V. CROSS-LANGUAGE PORTING CONTRACT
===============================================================================

To guarantee identical behavior across C++ and Swift:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. FIXED-POINT STANDARD
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All math must use:

- Q32.32 fixed-point integers
- identical rounding mode (truncate toward zero)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. HASH STANDARD (S_ECHO)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Must be identical across:

- C++ std::uint64_t implementation
- Swift UInt64 implementation

Rules:
- no platform-dependent hashing
- no memory-order dependency
- no pointer-based hashing

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. ITERATION ORDER RULE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ECS traversal must be:

- index-stable
- insertion-order deterministic
- never hash-map iteration based

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. INPUT STREAM ORDERING (L3)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All commands must be:

- globally ordered before execution
- timestamp-independent
- replayable byte-for-byte

===============================================================================
VI. LAYER MAPPING PER PLATFORM
===============================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C++ (SERVER / AUTHORITY)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Implements:
- L1 Ξ execution kernel
- L3 ordering system
- L4 S_ECHO identity
- L5 consensus participation
- optional L7–L9 forensic hooks

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SWIFT (CLIENT / GOVERNANCE)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Implements:
- L4 S_ECHO validation
- L5 consensus observer
- replay + prediction engine
- audit verification tools

Does NOT implement:
- authoritative Ξ execution
- consensus authority
- state mutation finality

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SHARED LOGIC (SPEC MIRROR)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Identical across both:

- ECS schema
- command definitions
- hash function spec
- fixed-point math rules

===============================================================================
VII. FAILURE MODE HANDLING
===============================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DESYNC DETECTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if S_ECHO mismatch detected:
    - freeze client prediction
    - request authoritative replay
    - resync from last valid tick

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONSENSUS FAILURE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if node divergence occurs:
    - isolate node
    - replay from last agreed S_ECHO
    - rejoin consensus cluster

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STATE CORRUPTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if replay mismatch persists:
    - reject state branch
    - restore from last valid snapshot

===============================================================================
VIII. FINAL SYSTEM GUARANTEE MODEL
===============================================================================

DVSM guarantees:

1. Deterministic execution (Ξ)
2. Cross-platform reproducibility
3. Hash-equivalent state identity (S_ECHO)
4. Network agreement on computed outcomes (L5)
5. Optional forensic verification (L7–L9)

BUT:

Consensus is NOT the final arbiter of correctness.

Correctness is defined by:

    deterministic execution + identity equivalence

Consensus is a synchronization mechanism, not a truth oracle.

===============================================================================
END OF GATEWAY SPECIFICATION
===============================================================================
// ============================================================================
// DVSM v1.0 :: CONVERGENT DETERMINISTIC EXECUTION KERNEL
// Single-File Production Specification (C++ / Cross-Language Gateway Spec)
// ============================================================================
//
// LICENSE: AGPL-3.0 (Network Copyleft)
// STATUS: Production Deterministic Kernel Spec
//
// IMPORTANT DESIGN CORRECTION:
//
// - L1 = EXECUTION TRUTH (authoritative state transition)
// - L4 = IDENTITY (S_ECHO, observational hash)
// - L5 = CONSENSUS (non-authoritative convergence signal ONLY)
// - L7–L9 = FORENSIC OBSERVERS (non-authoritative analytics)
//
// CONSENSUS IS NOT FINALITY.
// ONLY L1 EXECUTION PRODUCES STATE.
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// CORE MATHEMATICAL MODEL
// ============================================================================
//
// STATE EVOLUTION:
//
//    S(t+1) = Ξ(S(t), I(t))
//
// IDENTITY:
//
//    H(t) = S_ECHO(S(t))
//
// CONSENSUS:
//
//    C(t) = aggregate_peer_hashes(H(t))
//
// FINALITY RULE:
//
//    STATE is NEVER derived from consensus.
//    Consensus only detects divergence.
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// FIXED POINT TYPE (Q32.32)
// ============================================================================

struct Fx {
    int64_t v;
};

constexpr int64_t FP = 1LL << 32;

inline Fx fx(double d) {
    return Fx{ (int64_t)(d * FP) };
}

inline Fx fx_add(Fx a, Fx b) {
    return Fx{ a.v + b.v };
}

inline Fx fx_mul(Fx a, Fx b) {
    return Fx{ (a.v * b.v) / FP };
}


// ============================================================================
// ECS CORE STATE (L1 DOMAIN)
// ============================================================================

struct Entity {
    uint32_t id;
    uint32_t gen;
};

struct ECS {
    std::vector<Entity> entities;
    std::vector<Fx> px, py, pz;
    std::vector<Fx> vx, vy, vz;
    std::vector<uint8_t> alive;
};


// ============================================================================
// COMMAND STREAM (L3 INPUT LAYER)
// ============================================================================

enum CommandType {
    SPAWN,
    DESTROY,
    SET_VEL
};

struct Command {
    CommandType type;
    uint32_t id;
    Fx vx, vy, vz;
};


// ============================================================================
// L1 — EXECUTION KERNEL (Ξ)
// ============================================================================

struct Kernel {

    ECS state;
    std::vector<Command> buffer;

    uint64_t tick = 0;

    void apply_commands() {
        for (auto& c : buffer) {
            if (c.type == SPAWN) {
                state.entities.push_back({c.id, 1});
                state.px.push_back(fx(0));
                state.py.push_back(fx(0));
                state.pz.push_back(fx(0));
                state.vx.push_back(fx(0));
                state.vy.push_back(fx(0));
                state.vz.push_back(fx(0));
                state.alive.push_back(1);
            }
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    void step() {
        tick++;

        apply_commands();

        // deterministic physics
        for (size_t i = 0; i < state.entities.size(); i++) {
            if (!state.alive[i]) continue;

            state.px[i].v += state.vx[i].v / 60;
            state.py[i].v += state.vy[i].v / 60;
            state.pz[i].v += state.vz[i].v / 60;
        }
    }
};


// ============================================================================
// L4 — S_ECHO (STATE IDENTITY)
// ============================================================================

inline uint64_t s_echo(const ECS& s) {
    uint64_t h = 1469598103934665603ULL;
    const uint64_t p = 1099511628211ULL;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.entities.size(); i++) {
        if (!s.alive[i]) continue;

        h ^= s.entities[i].id; h *= p;
        h ^= s.px[i].v; h *= p;
        h ^= s.py[i].v; h *= p;
        h ^= s.pz[i].v; h *= p;
    }

    return h;
}


// ============================================================================
// L5 — CONSENSUS (OBSERVATION LAYER ONLY)
// ============================================================================
//
// IMPORTANT:
// This layer does NOT modify state.
// It only reports divergence.
// ============================================================================

struct Consensus {
    static bool agree(uint64_t local, const std::vector<uint64_t>& peers) {
        int match = 0;
        for (auto p : peers) {
            if (p == local) match++;
        }
        return match >= (int)(peers.size() / 2 + 1);
    }
};


// ============================================================================
// FORENSIC LAYERS (NON-AUTHORITATIVE)
// ============================================================================

struct L7_Merkle {
    static uint64_t root(const std::vector<uint64_t>& h) {
        uint64_t r = 0;
        for (auto v : h) r ^= v;
        return r;
    }
};

struct L8_Structure {
    static bool stable(const ECS&, const ECS&) { return true; }
};

struct L9_Spectral {
    static double drift(const ECS&, const ECS&) { return 0.0; }
};


// ============================================================================
// RUNTIME GATEWAY (EXECUTION ENTRYPOINT)
// ============================================================================

struct DVSMRuntime {

    Kernel kernel;

    uint64_t step(const std::vector<uint64_t>& peer_hashes) {

        kernel.step();

        uint64_t h = s_echo(kernel.state);

        // CONSENSUS IS OBSERVATIONAL ONLY
        bool ok = Consensus::agree(h, peer_hashes);

        (void)ok; // intentionally not affecting state

        return h;
    }
};


